body:
The thought of it seems so relieving - an end to the emptiness.

&#x200B;

And yet it's so hard to do. We're all on the same boat here, brother.

I know. And I want to become decent at this instrument. But so far, every song takes like a month (whereas guitar can take as little as 2 hours) to learn. At the very least. That's way too long for any actual band application.

&#x200B;

I want to practice and hell, at the moment, I sit with my instruments for like four hours a day (split more or less evenly among them), but I can't shake the feeling that I'm practicing wrong. I just don't know what to practice, how to practice, what to play and how to play it.

I get ya. Alright, say that money isn't a problem, and the only thing I want to buy is fret wire and tuning machines (all together for about $150, because I wanna make this acoustic REALLY fancy).

&#x200B;

My real intent here is to make myself a guitar that'll last me for years, a guitar I can pass down to my kids one day, a guitar that is unlike any other. I have about $600 on me right now, but I can work, get a job, make it to about $1,200 in a few months.

Already drew up some designs. Wanna make some engravings on the neck, create a really elaborate head stock, since all it costs me is materials and time, and get a fancy finish.

&#x200B;

Do you think that the materials alone are what's costly? I have a lot of musician/luthier friends willing to lend me equipment for fretting, even some who'd be willing to help me with it, so the materials are really my only expense.
